WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.001 --> 00:00:10.111
"Do Collagen Supplements Work for Skin Aging?"

00:00:10.231 --> 00:00:13.740
Collagen supplements have become
quite the trendy treatment

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:14.940
for aging skin,

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:18.120
available in an array of pills,
powders, and products

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:21.420
from bars and gummies
to collagen coffee and beer.

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:25.080
Social media is said to be
"inundated with paid ads

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:27.480
marketing
unsubstantiated claims."

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:31.440
What claims, if any,
can be substantiated?

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:34.560
The word collagen comes
from the Greek kola,

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:37.260
meaning "gum,"
and gen, meaning "producing."

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:41.880
Its gelling nature is
exemplified by Jell-O gelatin,

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:44.880
which is basically
just cooked collagen.

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:46.860
It is our most abundant protein

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:48.360
and the main
structural component

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:49.740
of most of the tissues
in our body.

00:00:49.740 --> 00:00:52.620
For example, up to three
quarters of our skin by weight

00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:54.180
may be pure collagen.

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:56.400
There are actually
dozens of different types,

00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:57.900
but they all share
a characteristic

00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:01.020
triple helix configuration
of coiled subunits.

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:03.600
What happens when we eat it?

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:07.080
If someone would have asked
for my educated guess

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:08.520
about taking collagen

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:12.000
before I took a deep dive
for my book How Not to Age,

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:16.380
I would have dismissed it
as witch-doctory woo-woo.

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:20.160
Eating skin for your face would
seem to make as much sense

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:22.080
as eating brains
to make yourself smarter

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:23.880
or eyeballs
to improve your vision.

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:26.040
After all,
when you eat proteins,

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:29.400
they are broken down into
their constituent amino acids,

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:32.520
which are then rebuilt into
any protein your body needs.

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:35.400
And, of all proteins to eat,

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:37.140
collagen is the only protein
in our food supply

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:38.760
that's truly incomplete.

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:41.460
That is, it's missing
an essential amino acid.

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:44.100
Other dietary proteins,
including all plant proteins,

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:46.800
contain all of
the essential amino acids--

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:49.560
in fact, that's why
they're called essential,

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:51.840
since animals like us
can't make them.

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:55.620
Collagen, though, is missing
the amino acid tryptophan.

00:01:55.620 --> 00:01:59.820
So, the only protein source that
you couldn't live on is Jell-O,

00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:03.240
since it effectively has
a protein quality score of zero.

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:06.480
But obviously that's
not all people eat;

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:08.100
so, significant
amounts of collagen

00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:10.560
could be incorporated into
the diet without keeling over,

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:12.540
but tryptophan is special.

00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:16.080
Randomizing people
to even a single meal

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:18.120
of a gelatin-based protein drink

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:20.940
can lead to memory impairments
within hours

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:24.300
due to
"acute tryptophan depletion,"

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:27.900
presumably due to a drop
in the brain of serotonin,

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:30.780
which is made from
tryptophan and doesn't just

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:33.000
regulate mood but learning
and memory as well.

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:35.760
So, the idea
of collagen supplements

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:37.020
never made sense to me,

00:02:37.020 --> 00:02:39.240
but the proof is in the pudding,

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:42.000
or rather, perhaps,
the Jell-O.

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:45.240
What does the science say?

00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:48.540
My assumption that
collagen proteins would get

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:51.600
completely broken down in
the digestive tract was wrong.

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:54.420
Feed people gelatin from
pig skin or chicken feet,

00:02:54.420 --> 00:02:56.820
and little pieces
of collagen particles

00:02:56.820 --> 00:02:59.040
can be found floating in
their bloodstream within hours.

00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:03.360
Though no further tracking has
been done in humans, in rodents,

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:05.340
collagen fragments
from gelatin consumption

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:08.160
can end up in
cartilage and skin.

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:10.680
Now, biologically,
there is no mechanism

00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:13.380
by which these fragments
could be directly incorporated

00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:14.340
into new collagen,

00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:16.800
but there may be an indirect way

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:19.380
collagen bits could boost
collagen deposition,

00:03:19.380 --> 00:03:21.360
as appears to happen
in rat skin.

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:23.520
What about in people?

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:27.180
Some of the collagen studies
lack control groups.

00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:31.020
I'm reminded of that vintage
Star Trek episode Mudd's Women,

00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:34.740
where Kirk successfully swaps
out a youth-giving supplement

00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:37.020
for a placebo
without the aliens noticing.

00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:40.620
Randomized controlled trials
are scarce,

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:44.580
but a systematic review
and meta-analysis

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:46.080
of the available data
showed that,

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:47.940
although outcomes were mixed,
on average,

00:03:47.940 --> 00:03:50.700
there were beneficial effects
for skin hydration,

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:52.320
elasticity, and roughness.

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:54.660
Despite some favorable results,

00:03:54.660 --> 00:03:56.520
much of the
dermatology community

00:03:56.520 --> 00:03:58.080
has remained skeptical,

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:01.320
suggesting the collagen
supplement industry may just be

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:04.260
flooding the zone with
poor quality, biased studies.

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:06.780
The studies are often funded

00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:08.760
by collagen supplement
manufacturers,

00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:11.760
and the overall quality of
evidence is considered to be

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:15.060
"limited, contradictory,"
"not particularly robust."

00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:17.940
A 2022 review entitled

00:04:17.940 --> 00:04:20.700
"Myths and media in oral
collagen supplementation

00:04:20.700 --> 00:04:22.080
for the skin, nails, and hair"

00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:25.320
in the Journal of Cosmetic
Dermatology concluded that

00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:27.060
"Dermatologists should be aware

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:29.940
of the unsubstantiated
proclamations of collagen

00:04:29.940 --> 00:04:32.460
made by companies
[which] surpass any evidence

00:04:32.460 --> 00:04:34.140
currently supported
by the literature."

00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:36.060
And, given
the insufficient evidence,

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:39.600
"collagen cannot be routinely
recommended at this time."

00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:42.660
Moreover, the evidence
is considered to be

00:04:42.660 --> 00:04:44.280
"particularly unconvincing"

00:04:44.280 --> 00:04:47.160
when compared to methods more
definitely shown to have

00:04:47.160 --> 00:04:48.960
a positive effect
on skin collagen,

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:51.720
such as sunscreen use,
smoking cessation,

00:04:51.720 --> 00:04:53.580
and a healthy diet.

